# Rust SDK MCP Development

Relevant docs:
- Rust SDK: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk
- Build MCP server: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/build-server
- Build MCP client: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/build-client
- TypeScript schema 2025-11-25: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/schema/2025-11-25/schema.ts
- Rust `skills` CLI crate: https://crates.io/crates/skills
- Rust `skills` library docs: https://docs.rs/skills/latest/skills/
- Rust skills rule knowledgebase: https://github.com/leonardomso/rust-skills

## Trigger Scenarios

Apply when reviewing, installing, testing, or planning Rust SDK components, MCP tools, resources, prompts, transports, server behavior, or client behavior.

## Required Controls

- Verify claims against upstream docs or local Rust code before teaching or accepting them.
- When adding or deploying agent skills for Rust work, use the native `skills` CLI protocol as the deployment source of truth and consult the Rust skills rule knowledgebase before creating new rules.
- Use real commands such as `cargo test`, `cargo check`, or repository-specific install commands only when the target exists.
- Record command, environment, exit code, and important stdout/stderr before calling a check verified.
- Use `blocked` or `unverified` when the Rust project, dependency, network, or install target is unavailable.

## No-Mock Gate

Do not create or accept mock installs, mock tests, fake logs, simulated exit codes, placeholder evidence, fabricated review results, or unsupported source claims. Missing evidence must be `blocked` or `unverified`.

## May Change

- Rust SDK review notes, install/test plans, component review reports, and Cursor operating artifacts for Rust workflows.

## May Not Change

- Upstream SDK claims without source citation.
- Completion evidence, install logs, or test results without running the real command.
- Per-lesson Rust artifacts unless explicitly requested.

## Verifiable Output

Return source URLs, local paths inspected, commands run, exit codes, findings, and remaining blocked or unverified evidence.
